1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,129
June 18, 2019.

2
00:00:02,129 --> 00:00:05,275
The eastern Pacific, off South America.

3
00:00:05,275 --> 00:00:08,825
A Coast Guard boat races alongside something

4
00:00:08,825 --> 00:00:11,325
that barely breaks the surface.

5
00:00:11,775 --> 00:00:12,988
A long, low hull.

6
00:00:12,988 --> 00:00:14,130
A small cockpit.

7
00:00:14,130 --> 00:00:15,272
An exhaust pipe.

8
00:00:15,272 --> 00:00:17,983
Almost everything else is under water.

9
00:00:18,433 --> 00:00:21,101
A boarding team member jumps onto the moving

10
00:00:21,101 --> 00:00:23,041
hull, and forces open the hatch.

11
00:00:23,491 --> 00:00:26,680
Inside, there are five suspected smugglers.

12
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:29,571
And more than 17,000 pounds of cocaine.

13
00:00:30,021 --> 00:00:32,952
Who builds a boat designed to hide in the waves?

14
00:00:32,952 --> 00:00:35,760
And how do you find one, on an ocean this big?

15
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:38,621
This is the story of the narco-subs.

16
00:00:41,221 --> 00:00:43,248
For years, they were mostly a rumor.

17
00:00:43,698 --> 00:00:46,468
Investigators heard about stealthy boats being

18
00:00:46,468 --> 00:00:48,275
built in the Colombian jungle.

19
00:00:48,275 --> 00:00:50,805
But none could be tied for certain to drug

20
00:00:50,805 --> 00:00:51,527
trafficking.

21
00:00:51,977 --> 00:00:54,314
That changes in November 2006.

22
00:00:54,314 --> 00:00:57,818
About 100 miles off Costa Rica, a Coast Guard

23
00:00:57,818 --> 00:01:01,867
crew spots three snorkels sticking out of the water.

24
00:01:01,867 --> 00:01:03,113
No diver's flag.

25
00:01:03,563 --> 00:01:06,727
Underneath is a homemade vessel with a crew of

26
00:01:06,727 --> 00:01:08,997
four, carrying 3 tons of cocaine.

27
00:01:08,997 --> 00:01:10,923
It becomes known as Bigfoot.

28
00:01:11,373 --> 00:01:14,093
The official name is a self-propelled

29
00:01:14,093 --> 00:01:15,784
semi-submersible. SPSS.

30
00:01:15,784 --> 00:01:17,695
It's not a true submarine.

31
00:01:17,695 --> 00:01:20,635
It rides with most of its hull under the

32
00:01:20,635 --> 00:01:24,016
surface, leaving only a cockpit and an exhaust

33
00:01:24,016 --> 00:01:25,560
pipe above the water.

34
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:29,003
They're built from wood and fiberglass at

35
00:01:29,003 --> 00:01:29,952
hidden sites.

36
00:01:29,952 --> 00:01:31,996
Roughly 50 to 100 feet long.

37
00:01:31,996 --> 00:01:34,624
Painted grayish blue, they're almost

38
00:01:34,624 --> 00:01:37,982
impossible to spot by eye, and hard to pick up

39
00:01:37,982 --> 00:01:38,639
on radar.

40
00:01:39,089 --> 00:01:41,958
The crews, about four people, are often

41
00:01:41,958 --> 00:01:45,195
recruited right off the dock, according to a

42
00:01:45,195 --> 00:01:48,946
writer who investigated the boats, speaking to NPR.

43
00:01:48,946 --> 00:01:52,551
He said a successful trip might pay about $3,000.

44
00:01:53,001 --> 00:01:55,778
In two thousand nine, he estimated the

45
00:01:55,778 --> 00:01:58,409
semi-subs were carrying about 30% of

46
00:01:58,409 --> 00:02:00,382
Colombia's cocaine exports.

47
00:02:00,832 --> 00:02:03,875
Building one costs an estimated half a million

48
00:02:03,875 --> 00:02:05,264
to a million dollars.

49
00:02:05,264 --> 00:02:08,109
The cocaine inside can be worth hundreds of

50
00:02:08,109 --> 00:02:08,704
millions.

51
00:02:09,154 --> 00:02:10,898
Typically, it makes one trip.

52
00:02:10,898 --> 00:02:11,799
Then it's sunk.

53
00:02:12,799 --> 00:02:14,527
The boats had one more feature.

54
00:02:14,977 --> 00:02:16,122
Scuttling valves.

55
00:02:16,122 --> 00:02:18,950
If the Coast Guard closes in, the crew can

56
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:19,623
open them.

57
00:02:19,623 --> 00:02:23,057
The hull floods and sinks, with the cocaine inside.

58
00:02:23,057 --> 00:02:24,471
The crew floats free.

59
00:02:24,921 --> 00:02:27,262
For prosecutors, that was a problem.

60
00:02:27,262 --> 00:02:30,058
To bring a drug case, they needed evidence.

61
00:02:30,058 --> 00:02:31,229
A bale of cocaine.

62
00:02:31,229 --> 00:02:34,350
And the evidence was at the bottom of the ocean.

63
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:36,614
In September 2008, U.S.

64
00:02:36,614 --> 00:02:39,216
forces reach two of them in time.

65
00:02:39,666 --> 00:02:42,542
On September 13, about 350 miles off

66
00:02:42,542 --> 00:02:45,977
Guatemala, the Navy frigate McInerney and a

67
00:02:45,977 --> 00:02:49,172
Coast Guard law enforcement team board a

68
00:02:49,172 --> 00:02:52,766
59-foot semi-submersible at night, before its

69
00:02:52,766 --> 00:02:54,124
crew can sink it.

70
00:02:54,124 --> 00:02:55,562
7 tons of cocaine.

71
00:02:55,562 --> 00:02:57,479
Four Colombian suspects.

72
00:02:57,929 --> 00:03:01,188
Days later, the cutter Midgett stops another

73
00:03:01,188 --> 00:03:02,595
one near Guatemala.

74
00:03:02,595 --> 00:03:04,447
15,000 pounds of cocaine.

75
00:03:04,447 --> 00:03:05,780
Four crew members.

76
00:03:06,230 --> 00:03:09,045
A Coast Guard captain later called those cases

77
00:03:09,045 --> 00:03:11,371
the straw that broke the camel's back.

78
00:03:11,821 --> 00:03:15,091
Congress passes the Drug Trafficking Vessel

79
00:03:15,091 --> 00:03:16,384
Interdiction Act.

80
00:03:16,384 --> 00:03:19,501
It's signed into law on October 13, 2008.

81
00:03:19,951 --> 00:03:22,682
Now, knowingly operating a stateless

82
00:03:22,682 --> 00:03:26,171
semi-submersible in international waters, with

83
00:03:26,171 --> 00:03:29,054
intent to evade detection, is a crime.

84
00:03:29,054 --> 00:03:30,191
Up to 15 years.

85
00:03:30,191 --> 00:03:31,557
Drugs or no drugs.

86
00:03:32,007 --> 00:03:34,839
Officials worried about more than cocaine.

87
00:03:34,839 --> 00:03:35,917
The head of U.S.

88
00:03:35,917 --> 00:03:38,817
Southern Command at the time, Admiral James

89
00:03:38,817 --> 00:03:41,244
Stavridis, put the question bluntly.

90
00:03:41,694 --> 00:03:44,685
The law does what it was written to do.

91
00:03:44,685 --> 00:03:47,752
In 2016, a Colombian man is convicted in

92
00:03:47,752 --> 00:03:50,896
Florida for a semi-submersible that sank.

93
00:03:50,896 --> 00:03:52,736
No drugs were recovered.

94
00:03:52,736 --> 00:03:55,497
He serves 41 months and is deported.

95
00:03:55,947 --> 00:03:58,881
In August 2020, more than five hundred

96
00:03:58,881 --> 00:04:02,433
nautical miles off Central America, the cutter

97
00:04:02,433 --> 00:04:04,364
Bertholf stops him again.

98
00:04:04,364 --> 00:04:07,530
This time, on a semi-sub with about 2,000

99
00:04:07,530 --> 00:04:09,152
kilograms of cocaine.

100
00:04:09,602 --> 00:04:11,047
The crew doesn't stop.

101
00:04:11,047 --> 00:04:13,741
After warning shots and disabling fire, a

102
00:04:13,741 --> 00:04:16,631
boarding officer jumps aboard, takes control

103
00:04:16,631 --> 00:04:19,325
of the engines, and pulls the fuel lines.

104
00:04:19,325 --> 00:04:21,559
There's already water in the hull.

105
00:04:21,559 --> 00:04:24,581
Prosecutors say it suggests an attempt to sink

106
00:04:24,581 --> 00:04:25,303
the vessel.

107
00:04:25,753 --> 00:04:26,751
The acting U.S.

108
00:04:26,751 --> 00:04:28,214
attorney in San Diego:

109
00:04:28,214 --> 00:04:31,140
“The Pacific Ocean is not a freeway for drug

110
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:32,004
traffickers.”

111
00:04:33,004 --> 00:04:35,549
Catching a boat that hides in the waves starts

112
00:04:35,549 --> 00:04:36,268
from the air.

113
00:04:36,718 --> 00:04:40,022
Patrol aircraft sweep the ocean with sensors

114
00:04:40,022 --> 00:04:41,600
and infrared cameras.

115
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:44,679
This is a CBP P-3, tracking a low-profile

116
00:04:44,679 --> 00:04:45,955
smuggling vessel.

117
00:04:46,405 --> 00:04:50,447
That case ended with 4,134 pounds of cocaine

118
00:04:50,447 --> 00:04:54,213
seized, worth almost $54 million dollars.

119
00:04:54,663 --> 00:04:57,774
The hunt is coordinated by Joint Interagency

120
00:04:57,774 --> 00:04:59,896
Task Force South, part of U.S.

121
00:04:59,896 --> 00:05:01,098
Southern Command.

122
00:05:01,098 --> 00:05:04,209
It pulls together the Coast Guard, the Navy,

123
00:05:04,209 --> 00:05:07,249
the Air Force, law enforcement agencies and

124
00:05:07,249 --> 00:05:08,381
partner nations.

125
00:05:08,831 --> 00:05:11,736
Once a target is found, a cutter closes in.

126
00:05:11,736 --> 00:05:13,222
A helicopter overhead.

127
00:05:13,222 --> 00:05:14,911
Small boats in the water.

128
00:05:15,361 --> 00:05:18,369
Coast Guard helicopters can disable a fleeing

129
00:05:18,369 --> 00:05:20,575
boat by shooting out its engines.

130
00:05:20,575 --> 00:05:23,382
A semi-sub is built not to be seen at all.

131
00:05:23,832 --> 00:05:27,619
By mid-2015, the Coast Guard counted 25 known

132
00:05:27,619 --> 00:05:31,406
semi-submersible interdictions in the eastern

133
00:05:31,406 --> 00:05:34,520
Pacific since that first one in 2006.

134
00:05:34,970 --> 00:05:37,102
Boarding a semi-sub is dangerous.

135
00:05:37,102 --> 00:05:39,880
The Coast Guard says its teams have limited

136
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:42,594
time inside, because the vessel could sink

137
00:05:42,594 --> 00:05:44,015
with them still in it.

138
00:05:44,465 --> 00:05:47,142
The cocaine comes out bale by bale, passed

139
00:05:47,142 --> 00:05:49,308
hand to hand into the small boats.

140
00:05:49,758 --> 00:05:53,112
Then it's hoisted aboard the cutter in cargo nets.

141
00:05:53,112 --> 00:05:55,996
The empty hull is often sunk as a hazard to

142
00:05:55,996 --> 00:05:56,734
navigation.

143
00:05:57,734 --> 00:06:01,718
Between June 2015 and March 2016, the Coast

144
00:06:01,718 --> 00:06:04,219
Guard stops five semi-subs.

145
00:06:04,669 --> 00:06:05,332
July 18.

146
00:06:05,332 --> 00:06:08,892
More than 200 miles south of Mexico, a Navy

147
00:06:08,892 --> 00:06:12,700
patrol plane spots a 40-foot semi-submersible.

148
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:15,101
The cutter Stratton moves in.

149
00:06:15,551 --> 00:06:18,391
Boarding teams pull out 275 bales.

150
00:06:18,391 --> 00:06:20,981
About 12,000 pounds of cocaine.

151
00:06:20,981 --> 00:06:24,406
At the time, the largest semi-submersible

152
00:06:24,406 --> 00:06:27,413
interdiction in Coast Guard history.

153
00:06:27,863 --> 00:06:30,904
It's Stratton's second semi-sub of the patrol.

154
00:06:30,904 --> 00:06:33,945
Since April, the crew has stopped 15 smuggling

155
00:06:33,945 --> 00:06:34,540
attempts.

156
00:06:34,990 --> 00:06:38,045
About 2,000 more pounds were left aboard to

157
00:06:38,045 --> 00:06:40,390
keep the vessel stable under tow.

158
00:06:40,390 --> 00:06:43,374
It sank in more than 13,000 feet of water.

159
00:06:43,824 --> 00:06:47,524
Six weeks later, the cutter Bertholf stops

160
00:06:47,524 --> 00:06:48,582
another one.

161
00:06:48,582 --> 00:06:50,079
More than 7 tons.

162
00:06:50,079 --> 00:06:54,132
Then, in March 2016, about 300 miles southwest

163
00:06:54,132 --> 00:06:57,304
of Panama, Bertholf catches a third.

164
00:06:57,304 --> 00:06:59,419
More than 12,800 pounds.

165
00:06:59,869 --> 00:07:03,831
Prosecutors later tied three semi-subs seized

166
00:07:03,831 --> 00:07:07,617
in July and August 2015, and March 2016, to

167
00:07:07,617 --> 00:07:09,554
the same organization.

168
00:07:10,004 --> 00:07:13,219
The case goes to the Panama Express Strike

169
00:07:13,219 --> 00:07:14,368
Force in Tampa.

170
00:07:14,368 --> 00:07:17,889
Agents from the DEA, FBI and Homeland Security

171
00:07:17,889 --> 00:07:20,645
Investigations, Coast Guard and Navy

172
00:07:20,645 --> 00:07:23,784
investigators, and Joint Interagency Task

173
00:07:23,784 --> 00:07:24,703
Force South.

174
00:07:25,153 --> 00:07:28,269
According to federal prosecutors, the group

175
00:07:28,269 --> 00:07:31,531
sent its semi-submersibles from Colombia into

176
00:07:31,531 --> 00:07:34,792
the Pacific, bound for Sinaloa cartel members

177
00:07:34,792 --> 00:07:36,097
in Oaxaca, Mexico.

178
00:07:36,547 --> 00:07:39,263
Its members built the fiberglass hulls and

179
00:07:39,263 --> 00:07:42,043
oversaw security at the construction sites.

180
00:07:42,043 --> 00:07:45,018
One installed the engines, recruited mechanics

181
00:07:45,018 --> 00:07:46,893
and taught crews to run them.

182
00:07:47,343 --> 00:07:51,528
That man, Jimmy Riascos-Riascos, also admitted

183
00:07:51,528 --> 00:07:55,621
he'd crewed a semi-sub that made it to Mexico

184
00:07:55,621 --> 00:07:56,713
in May 2015.

185
00:07:56,713 --> 00:08:00,625
In January 2020, he's sentenced to 24 years

186
00:08:00,625 --> 00:08:01,807
and 4 months.

187
00:08:02,257 --> 00:08:05,488
By September 2021, six more Colombians have

188
00:08:05,488 --> 00:08:08,944
pleaded guilty, including the man who admitted

189
00:08:08,944 --> 00:08:12,625
he was the organization's boss, known as Padrino.

190
00:08:13,625 --> 00:08:17,139
Another network used semi-subs to move cocaine

191
00:08:17,139 --> 00:08:20,730
to Guatemala, and on across the Mexican border.

192
00:08:20,730 --> 00:08:24,167
Its investor and organizer, a Colombian known

193
00:08:24,167 --> 00:08:27,528
as Guatala, was nicknamed in Colombian media

194
00:08:27,528 --> 00:08:29,973
the Prince of Semi-Submersibles.

195
00:08:29,973 --> 00:08:32,799
In May 2023, a federal judge in Tampa

196
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:35,855
sentenced him to 20 years and 10 months.

197
00:08:36,855 --> 00:08:38,305
The boats keep changing.

198
00:08:38,755 --> 00:08:42,195
In July 2010, Ecuadorian police and soldiers,

199
00:08:42,195 --> 00:08:45,711
working with the DEA, seize something new near

200
00:08:45,711 --> 00:08:47,316
the Colombian border.

201
00:08:47,316 --> 00:08:49,379
A fully submersible vessel.

202
00:08:49,829 --> 00:08:52,607
The DEA's regional director warned that it

203
00:08:52,607 --> 00:08:53,797
raised the stakes.

204
00:08:54,247 --> 00:08:55,877
The routes spread too.

205
00:08:55,877 --> 00:08:58,914
In July 2011, the cutter Seneca stops the

206
00:08:58,914 --> 00:09:02,173
first semi-sub the Coast Guard has caught in

207
00:09:02,173 --> 00:09:03,950
the Caribbean. It sinks.

208
00:09:03,950 --> 00:09:07,284
Weeks later, the wreck is found on sonar, and

209
00:09:07,284 --> 00:09:10,320
FBI divers carry out the first underwater

210
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:12,765
removal of drugs from a semi-sub.

211
00:09:13,215 --> 00:09:15,614
Two months later, the cutter Mohawk stops

212
00:09:15,614 --> 00:09:17,311
another one in the Caribbean.

213
00:09:17,761 --> 00:09:21,400
In November 2019, a homemade semi-submersible

214
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:24,878
turns up on the other side of the Atlantic.

215
00:09:24,878 --> 00:09:27,466
Off Galicia, in northwest Spain.

216
00:09:27,466 --> 00:09:31,429
Spanish authorities seize 3,000 kilos of cocaine.

217
00:09:31,429 --> 00:09:35,149
Officials call it the first narco-sub detected

218
00:09:35,149 --> 00:09:35,958
in Europe.

219
00:09:36,408 --> 00:09:39,046
In the Pacific, they keep coming.

220
00:09:39,046 --> 00:09:42,484
The Munro's June 2019 seizure was one of 14

221
00:09:42,484 --> 00:09:46,162
interdictions and disruptions by three cutters

222
00:09:46,162 --> 00:09:47,841
between May and July.

223
00:09:47,841 --> 00:09:50,399
Offloaded together in San Diego:

224
00:09:50,399 --> 00:09:53,197
more than 39,000 pounds of cocaine.

225
00:09:53,647 --> 00:09:57,667
In November 2023, the cutter Waesche stops a

226
00:09:57,667 --> 00:10:01,413
semi-sub carrying more than 5,500 pounds.

227
00:10:02,413 --> 00:10:04,546
Then comes the biggest year on record.

228
00:10:04,996 --> 00:10:08,941
In fiscal year 2025, the Coast Guard seized

229
00:10:08,941 --> 00:10:11,968
nearly 510,000 pounds of cocaine.

230
00:10:11,968 --> 00:10:14,169
The most in its history.

231
00:10:14,619 --> 00:10:18,243
In August 2025, it launches Operation Pacific

232
00:10:18,243 --> 00:10:21,947
Viper, a surge of cutters and helicopters into

233
00:10:21,947 --> 00:10:23,558
the eastern Pacific.

234
00:10:24,008 --> 00:10:27,595
On December 2, the Munro intercepts a heavily

235
00:10:27,595 --> 00:10:29,190
loaded go-fast boat.

236
00:10:29,190 --> 00:10:32,538
More than 20,000 pounds of narcotics, in a

237
00:10:32,538 --> 00:10:34,132
single interdiction.

238
00:10:34,132 --> 00:10:37,640
The largest at-sea seizure since March 2007.

239
00:10:38,090 --> 00:10:41,456
By February 2026, the Coast Guard reported

240
00:10:41,456 --> 00:10:45,062
more than 200,000 pounds of cocaine seized in

241
00:10:45,062 --> 00:10:47,306
six months of the operation.

242
00:10:47,756 --> 00:10:49,449
At the same time, the U.S.

243
00:10:49,449 --> 00:10:52,185
military has carried out lethal strikes on

244
00:10:52,185 --> 00:10:55,507
boats the administration says were smuggling drugs.

245
00:10:55,507 --> 00:10:57,526
Those strikes have been deadly.

246
00:10:57,526 --> 00:11:00,327
Their legality has been questioned, and the

247
00:11:00,327 --> 00:11:02,476
administration has defended them.

248
00:11:02,926 --> 00:11:05,614
For the Coast Guard, an interdiction still

249
00:11:05,614 --> 00:11:06,766
ends the same way.

250
00:11:06,766 --> 00:11:09,326
On a dock, in front of cameras, with the

251
00:11:09,326 --> 00:11:11,054
cocaine stacked on pallets.

252
00:11:11,054 --> 00:11:13,998
And, often, with the crews facing prosecution.

253
00:11:14,998 --> 00:11:18,051
In two decades, the narco-subs have gone from

254
00:11:18,051 --> 00:11:21,104
rumor, to Bigfoot, to a real submarine in the

255
00:11:21,104 --> 00:11:23,275
jungle, and across the Atlantic.

256
00:11:23,725 --> 00:11:26,576
Every one that's caught is months of work, and

257
00:11:26,576 --> 00:11:28,249
a fortune in cocaine, gone.

258
00:11:28,249 --> 00:11:30,232
And still, they keep turning up.

259
00:11:30,682 --> 00:11:32,574
The smugglers went under the waves.

260
00:11:32,574 --> 00:11:34,629
The Coast Guard learned to look there.

261
00:11:35,429 --> 00:11:37,378
Case file 003. Closed.

262
00:11:37,378 --> 00:11:40,655
The next file is already on the desk.
